Answer:  
Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:

- Relationship between father and newborn: A good or very good relationship likely reduces risk of postpartum depression; neutral or bad relationships may increase risk.  
- Angry after latest childbirth: Feeling angry after childbirth may indicate emotional distress, increasing risk of postpartum depression.  
- Addiction: Addictions (smoking, drinking, drugs) can be stressors or coping mechanisms linked with higher depression risk.  
- Monthly income before latest pregnancy: Low income or no income may correlate with higher stress and risk of depression; higher income may be protective.  
- Relationship with the newborn: A good or very good relationship suggests bonding and lower depression risk; bad or neutral may increase risk.  
- Need for Support: High or medium need for support suggests vulnerability and possible depression; low or no need suggests better mental health.  
- Age of newborn: Younger newborns (0 to 6 months) may be associated with higher risk due to adjustment period; older babies may correlate with decreased risk.  
- Number of the latest pregnancy: First or second pregnancy might have different stress levels; higher number could mean experience or more stress depending on context.  
- Age: Younger or older maternal age may affect risk; typically, very young mothers or older mothers might have higher risk.  
- Mode of delivery: Caesarean section may be linked with more physical and emotional stress, possibly increasing depression risk compared to normal delivery.  

---

Step 2. 10 different conditions for class "0" (No postpartum depression):  
- Angry after latest child birth is No  
- Need for Support is No  
- Relationship with the newborn is in [Good, Very good]  
- Relationship between father and newborn is in [Good, Very good]  
- Age of newborn is in [Older than 1.5 year, 1 year to 1.5 year, 6 months to 1 year]  
- Monthly income before latest pregnancy is in [No, 10000 to 20000, 20000 to 30000, More than 30000]  
- Addiction is No  
- Number of the latest pregnancy is >= 2  
- Age is within range [25, 35]  
- Mode of delivery is in [Caesarean Section, Normal Delivery]  

10 different conditions for class "1" (Yes postpartum depression):  
- Angry after latest child birth is Yes  
- Need for Support is in [Low, Medium]  
- Relationship with the newborn is Good (but combined with other risk factors)  
- Relationship between father and newborn is in [Neutral, Good] (not Very good)  
- Age of newborn is in [0 to 6 months, 6 months to 1 year]  
- Monthly income before latest pregnancy is in [No, Less than 5000, 5000 to 10000]  
- Addiction is No (based on examples, addiction is mostly No in both classes, so not a strong predictor here)  
- Number of the latest pregnancy is <= 2  
- Age is within range [22, 30]  
- Mode of delivery is in [Normal Delivery, Caesarean Section] (no clear difference, but present in examples)  

---

Summary:  
Class 0 (No postpartum depression) is characterized by no anger after childbirth, good or very good relationships with newborn and father, older newborn age, no or higher income, no addiction, more pregnancies, and middle maternal age.  
Class 1 (Yes postpartum depression) is characterized by anger after childbirth, some need for support (low or medium), younger newborn age, lower income, fewer pregnancies, and younger maternal age.

